fact WorkItem(id: "WI-163", description: some(value: "scaland resync — structural-op vs typeclass-op disambiguation. Prelude.registerStructuralOps in scaland/core/src/main/scala/anthill/load/Prelude.scala registers bare functor names (eq, neq, neg, and, or, not, lt, lte, gt, gte, div, mod, pow, arrow) as Operations under anthill.prelude — these are the names the Pratt parser produces from infix operators (`?a + ?b` → Fn(\"add\", ...)). When typeclass files load (eq.anthill defines anthill.prelude.Eq.eq, ordered.anthill defines Ordered.lt, etc.), every reference to `eq` in a rule body resolves ambiguously to BOTH anthill.prelude.eq AND anthill.prelude.Eq.eq, producing AmbiguousSymbol cascades during load. Same for neg (Float.neg vs prelude.neg). Fix must be a real disambiguation — downgrading Ambiguous to a warning is NOT acceptable. Design options: (a) drop registerStructuralOps entirely — let the parser emit functor names that resolve via the typeclass under the value's sort; (b) register structural ops as a distinct SymbolKind (e.g. SymbolKind.StructuralPlaceholder) that's filtered out during sort-method lookup and only consulted as a last-resort fallback; (d) wire structural ops to dispatch through fact-witness lookup at resolve time — eq(?a, ?b) where ?a: T resolves through Eq[T] fact to T's eq operation. Investigate how rustland handles this in rustland/anthill-core/src/intern.rs and resolve.rs — it does not produce AmbiguousSymbol on the same inputs. Mirror its mechanism. Acceptance: sbt test green; ParserIntegrationTest can load EmbeddedStdlib expanded with eq.anthill + ordered.anthill + numeric.anthill without AmbiguousSymbol(eq, ...) cascade."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-05-02T10:31:41Z"))
